; The count an off can lose. Winning 42 p. 10 (S-0023-a, S-0023-b, S-0023-c),
; p. 29 (S-0047-b) and p. 45 (S-0066-b); the bidding hands of Ch. 2 (Hands
; 1 to 11) and Hand 15.
; Claim, in our words: an off, a tile that is neither trump nor a double,
; belongs to two suits, and the setters can win it in either; on that trick
; they may play the count of either suit. A six off risks the 6-4, a blank off
; the 5-0, an ace off the 4-1, a deuce or trey off the 3-2, a four off the
; 6-4 and the 4-1, a five off the 5-5 and the 5-0. A count tile that is trump
; leaves its other suit: with fours trump a six or an ace off risks no count
; (Hand 15), with blanks trump a five off risks only the 5-5, with treys or
; deuces trump a deuce or trey off risks no 3-2; the setter knows the bidder
; budgeted nothing for it (p. 45). An off that is count risks itself (Hand
; 7's 5-0).
; Reading: (out o c): o is the bidder's off in the book's sense (Ch. 2: not
; trump, not a double; the prelude's (off o) is narrower, a tile that can
; still lose), and c is o itself if o is count, or a live count tile outside
; his hand that follows a suit o follows. The physics drops the trump count
; from its other suit. Which side the bid prices, the higher only, and once
; (S-0024-c, S-0024-d), is the bid's arithmetic (bidding/risk-budget.silas);
; which side his own double protects is off-exposed.silas.

(include book)

(query (out o c)
  (pattern
    (viewer b)
    (bidder b)
    (mine o)
    (not (trump-tile o))
    (not (double o))
    (count-tile o)
    (same o c))
  (pattern
    (viewer b)
    (bidder b)
    (mine o)
    (not (trump-tile o))
    (not (double o))
    (live c)
    (not (mine c))
    (count-tile c)
    (some (roles q) (in o q) (in c q))))
